<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Publius Servilius Vatia Isauricus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 134–<a href="/years/44-bce">44 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Publius Servilius Vatia Isauricus was a Roman general and statesman, consul in 79 BCE. As proconsul of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a> from 78 to 74 BCE he fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a> of the southern Anatolian coast, captured their strongholds in Lycia, including Phaselis and Olympus, and subdued the Isaurians, from whom he took his surname. He celebrated a triumph and lived to an old age as a senior senator.</p></div>
